    <body>
        <segment id="1" parent="10" relname="preparation">my search brought be to the same article.</segment>
        <segment id="2" parent="12" relname="span">I particularly agree with the idea that intuitive implies that an interface works the way that the user does,</segment>
        <segment id="3" parent="2" relname="elaboration">that no training is needed.</segment>
        <segment id="4" parent="14" relname="span">The article's conclusion is that something is intuitive</segment>
        <segment id="5" parent="13" relname="span">when it resembles something someone has already learned</segment>
        <segment id="6" parent="5" relname="antithesis">(so much for something new!).</segment>
        <segment id="7" parent="11" relname="span">But I really like the mathematical approach the best,</segment>
        <segment id="8" parent="7" relname="elaboration">where intuitiveness = uses readily transferred, existing skills.</segment>
        <group id="9" type="span"/>
        <group id="10" type="span" parent="9" relname="span"/>
        <group id="11" type="span" parent="10" relname="span"/>
        <group id="12" type="span" parent="15" relname="span"/>
        <group id="13" type="span" parent="4" relname="condition"/>
        <group id="14" type="span" parent="12" relname="elaboration"/>
        <group id="15" type="span" parent="11" relname="concession"/>
    </body>
